47767.8 gpu: world lighting-control control=$bumpmap2 kind=material-key state=refused declared=5 visible=5 resolved=5 missing=0 materials=5 triangles=13706 requested=0 effective=0 parsed=1 first=NATURE/BLENDCLIFFTOGRASS_ODYSSEY resource=materials/nature/dirtground001_height-ssbump.vtf reason=$bumpmap2 is layer 2's normal map: it is lerped into layer 1's normal by the vertex blend weight, so it is sampled only when --world-material-bump and --world-bump2 are both on; with either off it is refused, not silently replaced by layer 1's; 4/5 of those materials also declare $ssbump, so their second-layer texel is a bumpBasis weight and stays refused even with both switches on (--world-bump2-texture measures the layer with a diagnostic normal map instead)
47767.8 gpu: world lighting-control control=%detailtype kind=material-key state=refused declared=15 visible=14 resolved=0 missing=15 materials=14 triangles=19310 requested=0 effective=0 parsed=1 first=WOOD/WALL007A resource=materials/overlays/detail006.vmt reason=detail layers are the --world-detail switch: the detail material's $basetexture is sampled at the atlas uv scaled by $detailscale and combined with the albedo by $detailblendmode; with the switch off the layer is refused; 15 of 15 referenced detail VMTs are absent from this install, so the layer's own texture and blend mode cannot be resolved here; %detailtype is a key in the material that names the layer, not in the detail VMT, and is read above; 1 material names a %detailtype group (first=tf_forest_grass); %detailtype names a detail-prop group that VBSP consumes at map compile time (it emits prop_detail sprites and models there), so the key never reaches a runtime shader and has no consumer here
